<h2>The dispute&rsquo;s long tail</h2>
<p>TCIL won a tender of the Madhya Pradesh Rural Road Development Authority and sub-let the work back-to-back to Rajput Construction under an MOU of 18 October 2007. TCIL invoked arbitration on 7 April 2014; a sole arbitrator appointed unilaterally by TCIL passed an ex parte award on 15 May 2018. Rajput filed Section 34 objections on 27 May 2019, asserting it first received the award on 1 March 2019 through an RTI response. The Commercial Court set the award aside on 13 April 2023 on the unilateral-appointment ground — without deciding TCIL&rsquo;s objection that the petition was time-barred.</p>

<h2>The sequencing error</h2>
<p>The Division Bench&rsquo;s holding is about order of operations. Section 34(3) fixes a hard clock: three months from receipt of the award, with a grace period that is strictly capped — <em>&ldquo;The Court has the power to condone the delay only for a period of thirty days beyond the prescribed limitation period.&rdquo;</em> The general condonation power of Section 5 of the Limitation Act is excluded, a position settled through <strong>Consolidated Engineering</strong>, <strong>Singh Enterprises</strong>, <strong>Hongo India</strong> and <strong>Chhattisgarh SEB</strong>, all noticed in the judgment. A petition filed beyond three months and thirty days is dead on arrival, and nothing in it — however meritorious — can be examined.</p>
<p>That is why the Commercial Court&rsquo;s approach could not stand: <em>&ldquo;The Commercial Court erred in not deciding the issue of limitation at the first instance.&rdquo;</em> The unilateral-appointment ground under Section 12(5) — powerful after the Supreme Court&rsquo;s ineligibility jurisprudence — waits its turn behind the threshold question of when Rajput actually received the award, the fact on which the entire limitation computation hangs.</p>

<h2>Receipt of the award: the fact that starts the clock</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Event</th><th>Limitation consequence</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Delivery of a signed copy to the party (Section 31(5))</td><td>Starts the three-month period; delivery to an agent or counsel can raise disputes about effective receipt</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Knowledge of the award without delivery</td><td>Contested terrain — the challenger&rsquo;s asserted date of receipt (here, an RTI response of 1 March 2019) must be tested on evidence</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Three months from receipt</td><td>The primary window for filing Section 34 objections</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Thirty further days on sufficient cause</td><td>The absolute outer limit; beyond it, no condonation at all</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Practice consequences</h2>
<p>For award-holders, the judgment is a reminder to prove delivery meticulously — a signed copy served under Section 31(5), with acknowledgment — because a fuzzy delivery record hands the challenger its limitation argument. For challengers, the lesson is the mirror image: the date of receipt must be pleaded and provable, and the petition filed within the unforgiving window, since even a strong ineligibility ground cannot resurrect a late petition. The remitted proceedings, with appearance fixed for 14 December 2026, will decide the receipt question first.</p>
<div class="note">The judgment does not decide whether the challenge was in time or whether the unilaterally appointed arbitrator&rsquo;s award survives — both questions stand remitted. It settles the sequence: limitation first, merits after.</div>
